<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import Draft from './lib/Draft.abide'
import TokenWindow from './lib/TokenWindow.abide'
import draftSource from './lib/Draft.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import tokenWindowSource from './lib/TokenWindow.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import undoStackSource from './lib/undoStack.ts' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <p>The window is an array the value holds. You append to it and trim it in one
    <code>patch</code>, and how much it keeps is a line of your code rather than an option of
    ours.</p>

    <Section title="A window is an array trimmed in the patch that appends">
        <Example
            id="tail-window"
            title="`patch` with a trim — a window onto the recent past"
            summary="A pane over an answer arriving one token at a time. Press Next token a few times and watch the top of the list."
            files={{
                'lib/TokenWindow.abide': tokenWindowSource,
            }}
        >
            <TokenWindow />
        </Example>

        <p><em>1 patch, 2 lines in it</em> — the push and the trim, against the array the
        hand-written arm trims and the list it rebuilds per token.</p>

        <p><code>patch</code> hands you the held array and mutates it in place (5.7), so the push
        and the trim are one write and readers wake once for both. abide puts no cap on the array,
        so the trim is the condition you write: a count here, an age or a byte total wherever that
        is what bounds it. The latest value is <code>token.at(-1)</code>, and
        <code>{'{#for line of token}'}</code> is the window.</p>

        <p><code>by: 'at'</code> is what keeps the trim cheap. A keyed <code>{'{#for}'}</code> moves
        the rows it keeps (32.29), so a token adds one row and drops one, and the three in between
        are not touched. Without <code>by</code> the rows are positional (32.13): a
        <code>shift</code> moves every value up one place, and every row is rewritten. Give each
        value an id when it is appended, and declare <code>by</code> on the value that holds
        them.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/patch-a-list-from-a-live-feed')}>Patching a list</a> ·
        <a href={url('/templates/repeat-markup-over-a-list')}>Lists</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A value keeps no past of its own">
        <p>Every <code>Reactive</code> holds its standing value and nothing before it. Where you
        want the ones before it, hold them: make the value the array, and append where you
        write.</p>

        <p>A reader cannot build the window for you. A <code>watch</code> runs once per turn, and a
        burst of writes in one turn is one run (12.10), so a window a reader assembles misses
        whatever landed in the same turn. The writer sees every
        value, which is why the window is built where the write is.</p>

        <p>A channel is this same shape with many writers — a <code>reactive</code> over an array
        that everyone connected reads, trimmed the same way.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/let-anything-publish-and-anything-read')}>Channels</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="An undo stack is history the app keeps">
        <Example
            id="tail-undo"
            title="An undo stack is history the app keeps"
            summary="A composer draft with an Undo beside it. Type a few edits, then press Undo more than once."
            files={{
                'lib/undoStack.ts': undoStackSource,
                'lib/Draft.abide': draftSource,
            }}
        >
            <Draft />
        </Example>

        <p>A window is the recent past of a list many readers follow. An undo stack is the private
        past of one value, walked by the one reader typing into it, and the value itself stays a
        string. The past is an array the app pushes into — here from a <code>watch</code> over the
        draft. abide leaves the stack to you, because what a stack should coalesce, discard and
        restore differs per app.</p>

        <p>Two things decide how you build it.</p>

        <p>Each entry has to be a distinct object, or every restore hands back the value already on
        screen. A string already is one. A record is one when it is written through a member path,
        because that write copies down the path (31.8).</p>

        <p>And <strong>an undo is itself a write</strong>, so the <code>watch</code> pushes it onto
        the same array — walk backwards through an array you are also appending to and the second
        step reads the first one back. So the position lives over a snapshot: take one when a run
        begins and move an index over it, which is the <code>run</code> and <code>at</code> pair in
        <code>undoStack.ts</code>.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/do-something-when-a-value-changes')}>Watching values</a> ·
        <a href={url('/templates/read-and-write-a-value-by-name')}>Values by name</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A copy per write is the wrong shape for a stream of items">
        <p>A write through a member path copies down that path and then sets, so the reference moves
        and readers wake (31.8). It costs a copy per write, so it is the wrong shape for items
        arriving one at a time:</p>

        <Code language="ts" caption="shared" code={`for (const row of incoming) rows.push(row)   // O(n²) — a copy per row`} />

        <p><code>patch</code> is the escape. It mutates the array it already holds rather than
        copying it (5.7), so a batch is one call — <code>rows.patch((list) =&gt;
        list.push(...incoming))</code> — and one wake, however many rows it brought.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/templates/read-and-write-a-value-by-name')}>Values by name</a> ·
        <a href={url('/values/patch-a-list-from-a-live-feed')}>Patching a list</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`ABIDE_MAX_STREAM_BUFFER_SIZE` caps a transcript in memory">
        <p>A window is counted in values. The thing that can actually run away is bytes, and one
        variable caps it wherever a stream's transcript is held:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th></th><th></th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>ABIDE_MAX_STREAM_BUFFER_SIZE</code></td><td>bytes, default <code>Infinity</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>It governs a seed buffer entry's transcript: an entry past it stops holding the stream,
        and the browser's call runs the handler again and says so on <code>abide:hydrate</code>
        (36.18).</p>

        <p>A seed buffer entry needs no other bound: it is single-use and lives seconds, so the
        exposure is request rate multiplied by transcript size, and size is the only dimension that
        can grow. A count ceiling would evict an entry a document in flight is about to claim, which
        is the one failure the buffer exists to avoid.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/reference/configuration')}>Configuration</a> ·
        <a href={url('/pages/send-the-page-before-the-data-lands')}>Streaming HTML</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/values/let-anything-publish-and-anything-read')}>Channels</a> — the
            same window, written by many</li>
            <li><a href={url('/data/send-data-as-it-arrives')}>Streaming data</a> — where chunks
            come from</li>
            <li><a href={url('/ship/watch-a-room-from-the-terminal')}>Watch a channel</a> — a
            channel followed from a shell</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
